---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import AverageCalculator from '@/components/tools/math/AverageCalculator.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="average-calculator">
  <AverageCalculator client:load />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How to use it</h2>
    <p>
      Paste or type your numbers into the box. Commas, spaces, tabs, semicolons, pipes and newlines are
      all separators, so a column copied from a spreadsheet or a CSV file works without cleanup. The
      average calculator lists each entry that is not a number, so a stray header row cannot change your
      result without notice.
    </p>
    <p>
      All statistics update as you type. Use the copy buttons to copy the whole summary or the sorted
      list.
    </p>

    <h2>Mean, median and mode: when each one is right</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Measure</th>
            <th scope="col">How it is found</th>
            <th scope="col">Best used when</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Mean</td>
            <td>Sum ÷ count</td>
            <td>The data is roughly symmetric with no extreme outliers</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Median</td>
            <td>Middle value of the sorted list</td>
            <td>The data is skewed: incomes, house prices, response times</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Mode</td>
            <td>The most frequent value</td>
            <td>The data is categorical or you want the most common case</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Midrange</td>
            <td>(min + max) ÷ 2</td>
            <td>A rough centre when you only have the extremes</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      A classic example: nine people who earn 30,000 and one who earns 500,000 have a mean income of
      77,000 and a median of 30,000. The median describes the typical person much better, so official
      statistics usually give it for income and property prices.
    </p>

    <h2>Reading the other numbers</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Range: the distance from smallest to largest. It is simple, but one outlier controls it.</li>
      <li>Q1 and Q3: the values a quarter and three-quarters of the way through the sorted data.</li>
      <li>Interquartile range: Q3 − Q1, the spread of the middle half. It is much more robust than the range.</li>
      <li>Standard deviation: the typical distance of a value from the mean. This tool shows the sample version.</li>
      <li>Mean absolute deviation: the average distance from the mean, without squaring. It is easier to interpret and harder to use in algebra.</li>
      <li>Geometric mean: the correct average for growth rates and ratios. It is defined only when all values are positive.</li>
      <li>Harmonic mean: the correct average for rates such as speed over a fixed distance. It is defined only when no value is zero.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>How the median handles even counts</h2>
    <p>
      With an odd number of values, the median is the one value in the middle position. With an even
      count, there are two middle values and the median is their average: the median of 3, 7, 9, 12 is
      (7 + 9) ÷ 2 = 8. The step list shows the sorted data and the positions used, so you can check the
      number by eye.
    </p>

    <h2>When there is no mode, and when there are several</h2>
    <p>
      If each value appears exactly once, the data set has no mode, and the tool says so. It does not
      return the first value. If several values tie for the highest frequency, the tool reports all of
      them. A data set can be bimodal or multimodal, and that is often its most interesting property.
    </p>

    <h2>Which average for which job</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Exam marks: the mean, unless a few zeros from absent students pull it down.</li>
      <li>Salaries and house prices: always the median.</li>
      <li>Annual investment returns: the geometric mean. The arithmetic average of +50% and −50% suggests break-even, but the real result is a 25% loss.</li>
      <li>Average speed over a route: the harmonic mean of the leg speeds when the distances are equal.</li>
      <li>Shoe sizes to stock: the mode. Nobody wants the average shoe.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Performance and privacy</h2>
    <p>
      The calculation is one pass over your data plus one sort, so the browser handles tens of
      thousands of values at once. The frequency table lists the 30 most common values to keep the
      page fast. Nothing is uploaded, and the tool works with the network disconnected.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
